fuzzy.library/FuzzyIndexPath                      fuzzy.library/FuzzyIndexPath

   NAME
        FuzzyIndexPath -- Where the index is (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        length = FuzzyIndexPath(buf, size)
        D0                      A0   D0

        LONG FuzzyIndexPath(STRPTR, LONG);

   FUNCTION
        Puts the index's place into buf: the ENV: setting FuzzyFind/Index,
        else S:FuzzyFind.index (FUZZY_INDEX). Every FuzzyFind program and
        fuzzy.library itself (a NULL name) use this place.

   INPUTS
        buf  - where to put it
        size - its size

   RESULT
        length - the length put there (0 if size < 2)

   SEE ALSO
        FuzzyOpenIndex](#fuzzyopenindex), FuzzyNewIndex


fuzzy.library/FuzzyNewIndex                        fuzzy.library/FuzzyNewIndex

   NAME
        FuzzyNewIndex -- Start writing an index (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        writer = FuzzyNewIndex(name, error)
        D0                     A0    A1

        FuzzyWriter *FuzzyNewIndex(CONST_STRPTR, LONG *);

   FUNCTION
        Opens NAME.new to write a new index into; FuzzyEndIndex() renames
        it to NAME, so readers never see half an index.

   INPUTS
        name  - the index's file, or NULL for FuzzyIndexPath()'s place
        error - FERR_OK, or why it failed; may be NULL

   RESULT
        writer - the writer, or NULL (FERR_NOFILE: NAME.new cannot be made;
                 FERR_NOMEM)

   SEE ALSO
        FuzzyAddVolume](#fuzzyaddvolume), FuzzyAddEntry, FuzzyEndIndex


fuzzy.library/FuzzyNextEntry                      fuzzy.library/FuzzyNextEntry

   NAME
        FuzzyNextEntry -- The next entry of an index (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        ok = FuzzyNextEntry(index, entry)
        D0                 A0     A1

        LONG FuzzyNextEntry(struct FuzzyIndex *, struct FuzzyEntry *);

   FUNCTION
        Reads the next entry into entry: Path, PathLen, NameOffset, Type
        (FET_FILE, FET_DRAWER, FET_LINK), Prot, Size, Date.

   INPUTS
        index - from FuzzyOpenIndex()
        entry - filled in

   RESULT
        ok - TRUE, or FALSE at the end (or where the file is cut short or damaged)

        Notes: Path is the library's and changes with the next call: copy
        it to keep it.

   SEE ALSO
        FuzzyRewind](#fuzzyrewind), FuzzySearch


fuzzy.library/FuzzyOpenIndex                      fuzzy.library/FuzzyOpenIndex

   NAME
        FuzzyOpenIndex -- Open an index (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        index = FuzzyOpenIndex(name, error)
        D0                     A0    A1

        struct FuzzyIndex *FuzzyOpenIndex(CONST_STRPTR, LONG *);

   FUNCTION
        Opens an index and reads its header. The public fields: Count
        (entries), Created (seconds since 1978-01-01), Volumes.

   INPUTS
        name  - the index's file, or NULL for FuzzyIndexPath()'s place
        error - FERR_OK, or why it failed; may be NULL

   RESULT
        index - the index, or NULL: FERR_NOFILE (not there: run updatedb),
                FERR_NOTINDEX, FERR_VERSION (a format this library does not
                know), FERR_NOMEM

        Notes: Each task opens its own.

   SEE ALSO
        FuzzyNextEntry](#fuzzynextentry), FuzzySearch, FuzzyCloseIndex


fuzzy.library/FuzzyPrepare                          fuzzy.library/FuzzyPrepare

   NAME
        FuzzyPrepare -- Prepare a pattern (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        pattern = FuzzyPrepare(text, flags)
        D0                     A0    D0

        FuzzyPattern *FuzzyPrepare(CONST_STRPTR, ULONG);

   FUNCTION
        Makes a pattern from what was typed, to score any number of texts
        with. Words are split at spaces (up to 16); case and accents are
        folded away unless the flags say otherwise.

   INPUTS
        text  - the pattern, Latin-1
        flags - 0, or any of:
                  FPF_CASE     case matters
                  FPF_ACCENTS  accents matter
                  FPF_PLAIN    each word as one piece of text, no gaps
                  FPF_TEXT     the texts are not paths (no file-name bonus;
                               a word after a space counts more)

   RESULT
        pattern - the pattern, or NULL (text NULL, out of memory)

   SEE ALSO
        FuzzyScore](#fuzzyscore), FuzzyFreePattern


fuzzy.library/FuzzyRewind                            fuzzy.library/FuzzyRewind

   NAME
        FuzzyRewind -- Back to an index's first entry (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        FuzzyRewind(index)
                    A0

        VOID FuzzyRewind(struct FuzzyIndex *);

   FUNCTION
        The next FuzzyNextEntry() gives the first entry again.

   INPUTS
        index - from FuzzyOpenIndex()

   SEE ALSO
        FuzzyNextEntry](#fuzzynextentry)


fuzzy.library/FuzzyScore                              fuzzy.library/FuzzyScore

   NAME
        FuzzyScore -- How well a pattern matches a text (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        score = FuzzyScore(pattern, text, len, pos, maxpos)
        D0                 A0       A1    D0   A2   D1

        LONG FuzzyScore(FuzzyPattern *, CONST_STRPTR, LONG, UWORD *, LONG);

   FUNCTION
        Scores the text against the pattern as fzf does (its v1 way, path
        scheme): every word's letters in order; bonuses for the start of
        words, letters in a row and the file name; penalties for gaps. All
        words must match; their scores add up.

   INPUTS
        pattern - from FuzzyPrepare()
        text    - the text, Latin-1
        len     - its length, or -1 for up to its 0 (at most 65535 used)
        pos     - where to put each matched letter's position, or NULL
        maxpos  - how many pos has room for

   RESULT
        score - higher is better; 0 is no match; 1 for an empty pattern

        Notes: The positions are in pattern order, word after word: sort
        them to draw.

   SEE ALSO
        FuzzyPrepare](#fuzzyprepare), FuzzySearch


fuzzy.library/FuzzySearch                            fuzzy.library/FuzzySearch

   NAME
        FuzzySearch -- The best matches in an index (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        results = FuzzySearch(index, pattern, flags, max)
        D0                    A0     A1       D0     D1

        struct FuzzyResults *FuzzySearch(struct FuzzyIndex *, CONST_STRPTR, ULONG, LONG);

   FUNCTION
        Scores every entry and keeps the max best, best first (ties: the
        shorter path, then the earlier entry). Results: Count hits in
        Hits[] (Path, PathLen, NameOffset, Score, Type, Prot, Size, Date;
        the paths are copies), Matched (how many matched in all). Rewinds
        the index.

   INPUTS
        index   - from FuzzyOpenIndex()
        pattern - as for FuzzyPrepare()
        flags   - FPF_ flags, and FSF_FILES, FSF_DRAWERS (only those),
                  FSF_NAME (match only the name)
        max     - how many to keep (1 or more)

   RESULT
        results - the results, or NULL (out of memory, max < 1)

        Notes: It reads the whole index, so it takes longer the bigger the
        index and the slower the processor and the drive. Entries missing
        a letter of the pattern are skipped quickly (a letter
        fingerprint).

   SEE ALSO
        FuzzyFreeResults](#fuzzyfreeresults), FuzzyScore


fuzzy.library/FuzzyVolume                            fuzzy.library/FuzzyVolume

   NAME
        FuzzyVolume -- A volume an index covers (0.1).

   SYNOPSIS
        name = FuzzyVolume(index, n, date)
        D0                 A0     D0 A1

        CONST_STRPTR FuzzyVolume(struct FuzzyIndex *, LONG, ULONG *);

   FUNCTION
        Returns the n-th volume's name (without the colon) and its date.

   INPUTS
        index - from FuzzyOpenIndex()
        n     - 0 to Volumes - 1
        date  - set to the volume's date; may be NULL

   RESULT
        name - read-only, or NULL for n out of range

   SEE ALSO
        FuzzyAddVolume](#fuzzyaddvolume)


fuzzy.library/--the-arexx-entry--            fuzzy.library/--the-arexx-entry--

   NAME
        The ARexx entry -- The library's first entry (offset -30, before
            FuzzyPrepare) is not a C call: it is the function ARexx calls
            for ADDLIB('fuzzy.library', 0, -30, 0), with a RexxMsg in A0.
            It returns an error code in D0 and the result string in A0
            (and A1). The ARexx functions are in the guide.

